-- Liftback: the game's own car, as data (docs/game/features/car-model.md, T-111).
--
-- The 1980s liftback modelled in Blender for this game (the gray model, approved
-- 2026-11-10), exported as `assets/car-ae86/car-ae86.fbx` and imported into DR-Lane3 as
-- `Workspace.car-ae86`. It was read out of that place into this file so every place can
-- build it: 168 mesh pieces, each `<Part>_<Surface>`, and the 72 parts they belong to, 55
-- of which move. `Shared/Cars/MeshRig` turns this into a v2 `Shell` through `Rig.assemble`
-- (CONTRACTS.md "The car rig"); `Shared/CarModel` builds it as the body `liftback`.
--
-- Everything is in the game's car axes (X = the car's right, Y = up, -Z = forward), in
-- studs, with the origin ON THE GROUND midway between the axles -- not yet the car's frame,
-- which is `RideHeight` up: `MeshRig` subtracts it, so the ride height stays the config's.
-- The model's wheelbase is 9.6 and its track 5.6, the game's own, so it is built at scale 1
-- (`MeshRig` scales by `Wheelbase / measured.wheelbase` if the config ever differs).
--
--   surfaces  one entry per surface: the stock look (the user's reference, 2026-11-10:
--             white over black, black wheels and trim, dark glass, amber indicators, red
--             tail lamps, warm headlights and yellow fogs) and the `Paint` role it carries
--   groups    each moving part: its kind, pivot, axis and travel (`pivots-gray2.json`,
--             through `car-ae86.json`), in the axes above. A part not listed here is the
--             static body; `into` puts a static part on a moving one (the hatch's glass)
--   pieces    { name, mesh asset id, size x, y, z, centre x, y, z }. Every piece is turned
--             half a turn about Y (the importer read the file facing +Z)
--   points    the driver's seat (hips), where the driver steps out (an estimate), and the
--             nine lowest points of the underside, for the suspension
--
-- The meshes were uploaded by the user's import; they are this game's own art.

